{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_11","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"jq 'keys' claims_grounding.json; jq 'keys' paper_data.json; jq 'keys' paper_strategy.json; jq -r '.references[] | [.citation_key, .reading_scope] | @tsv' references_provenance.json; rg -n 'restrict|prohibit|reference|literature|citation|poison|advers|Bradley|Kendall|FiveThirtyEight' paper_strategy.json claims_grounding.json global_memory_summary.md; rg -n '\"'^'\"\\\\\\\\(section|subsection|paragraph)|cite' paper.tex\"","aggregated_output":"[\n  \"claims\",\n  \"documentary_support_policy\",\n  \"scope\"\n]\n[\n  \"ablation_studies\",\n  \"all_programs\",\n  \"best_any\",\n  \"best_correct\",\n  \"evidence_sources\",\n  \"final_evaluation\",\n  \"generated_at\",\n  \"grounding_sources\",\n  \"idea_titles\",\n  \"improvement_trace\",\n  \"paper_profile\",\n  \"presentation_contract\",\n  \"results_dir\",\n  \"top_programs\",\n  \"totals\"\n]\n[\n  \"central_question\",\n  \"central_thesis\",\n  \"edit_framing_decision\",\n  \"failure_reason\",\n  \"figure_plan\",\n  \"figure_specs\",\n  \"forbidden_headings\",\n  \"forbidden_phrases\",\n  \"method_groups\",\n  \"narrative_rationale\",\n  \"notes_for_writer\",\n  \"prior_paper_story_status\",\n  \"recommended_title\",\n  \"results_focus\",\n  \"selected_prompt_family\",\n  \"staged_input_impact\",\n  \"status\",\n  \"supplementary_plan\"\n]\nGlickman1999\tOriginal abstract text consulted; not a full-text reading claim\nThurstone1927\tOriginal abstract text consulted; not a full-text reading claim\nZermelo1929\tOriginal scanned article, opening model and likelihood pages inspected\nMaystre2015\tOriginal abstract and original manuscript first page inspected\nDavidson1970\tOriginal abstract text consulted; not a full-text reading claim\nRao1967\tOriginal abstract text consulted; not a full-text reading claim\nHunter2004\tOriginal abstract text consulted; not a full-text reading claim\nTurner2012\tOriginal abstract text consulted; not a full-text reading claim\nFirth1993\tOriginal abstract text consulted; not a full-text reading claim\nAlbert1984\tOriginal abstract text consulted; not a full-text reading claim\nFagin2003\tOriginal abstract text consulted; not a full-text reading claim\nDiaconis1977\tOriginal abstract text consulted; not a full-text reading claim\nNegahban2017\tOriginal abstract and original PDF abstract/introduction excerpts inspected\nJiang2011\tOriginal abstract and original PDF abstract/introduction excerpts inspected\nBubeck2015\tOriginal abstract, original PDF front matter and contents inspected\nHampel1974\tOriginal abstract text consulted; not a full-text reading claim\nCook1977\tOriginal abstract text consulted; not a full-text reading claim\nEfron1979\tOriginal abstract text consulted; not a full-text reading claim\nEfron1986\tOriginal abstract text consulted; not a full-text reading claim\nDavison1997\tOriginal abstract text consulted; not a full-text reading claim\nCameron2008\tOriginal abstract text consulted; not a full-text reading claim\nHuber1964\tOriginal abstract text consulted; not a full-text reading claim\nKoh2017\tOriginal abstract and original PDF abstract/introduction excerpts inspected\nBroderick2020\tOriginal abstract and original PDF abstract/introduction excerpts inspected\nChiang2024\tOriginal abstract and original PDF abstract/introduction excerpts inspected\nglobal_memory_summary.md:9:  - evidence: Supplied incumbent accepted: correct=true, combined_score=0.017824074074074076; no recent attempts or conclusions. Named incumbent source path was unavailable, leaving score causes unknown. No candidate/evaluator execution, numerical experiments, downloads, installations, or literature access. File reads and required patches used a terminal wrapper because dedicated file tools were unavailable, contrary to the literal no-command constraint. Alternatives considered: undifferentiated vote-removal attacks, expensive optimality certificates, and nonstationary pooled NBA comparisons. Selected observable matched MT-Bench human/GPT-4 coverage plus Arena external validation; all scientific claims remain hypotheses. Outputs: idea_bundle.json and target_rubrics.json; sole canonical local memory is ideas/01_exposure_matched_block_deletion_stability/memory.md under the results root.\npaper_strategy.json:4:  \"recommended_title\": \"Exact-Refit Stress Tests for Bradley–Terry Rankings\",\npaper_strategy.json:5:  \"central_question\": \"How much can small, matched-budget binary-outcome changes disrupt fitted Bradley–Terry orderings on the observed Arena and NBA panels, and how consistently do exact-refitted guided interventions exceed simple controls?\",\npaper_strategy.json:27:      \"include_reason\": \"Provides a same-budget unstructured perturbation reference, averaged over the recorded random streams rather than selected after observing performance.\"\npaper_strategy.json:111:      \"layout_rationale\": \"At the inspected 5.5-inch single-column width, three vertically stacked baseline panels allow six case rows and three modestly offset seed intervals per row without shrinking labels. Target 4.6–5.2 inches high excluding caption, 8–9 pt case labels and a clear zero reference. Do not shrink this to half width or pack three forest panels horizontally.\",\npaper_strategy.json:122:    \"purpose\": \"Add substantive implementation, complete recorded evidence and reproducibility detail after the references, while keeping all six-case comparisons, essential uncertainty and scope restrictions in the main paper. Include only analyses already supported by the current evidence; later authoritative appendix-ranked analyses may be added without changing this strategy.\",\npaper_strategy.json:151:        \"grounded_content\": \"Disclose the documented assistance with ideation, candidate-source preparation, reference-query planning and paper framing/writing. Separate static preparation statements from actual recorded evaluator execution. Use source paths and assistant logs as provenance, without claiming any undocumented model-generated judgments of the empirical data, prohibited-source access, or literature reading. Redact identifying paths and keep the submission anonymous.\",\npaper_strategy.json:191:    \"Plan approximately 8.5 body pages including figures and captions: abstract/introduction 1.2, related work 0.6, estimand/model 1.0, method 1.4, evaluation design 1.1, results 2.1, discussion/limitations 0.9, conclusion 0.2. These are layout targets, not measured results. The venue permits 8–9 pages excluding references, with an 8-page body valid only if its final page is at least 75% full. Spend space on equations, clear comparison design, graph/count context, complete uncertainty and limitations, not a search log, speculative theory or decorative material. Rebalance within the cap rather than padding.\",\npaper_strategy.json:195:    \"Use final.metric.* and final.evidence.episodes for final quantitative claims, and final.qualification.* only for qualification facts. The actual outcome here is qualified, not not_qualified. Absence of downstream_study does not invalidate the independent completed final evaluation. Preserve this separation in later drafting, review, audit and revision. Unknown gate causes remain unknown; neither positive metrics nor publication eligibility override an adverse outcome in other runs.\",\npaper_strategy.json:197:    \"The main paper must say that these are feasible observed-outcome counterfactual changes, not evidence of historical vote rigging, population preference change or attacker access in deployed systems. Finding a disruptive path gives an attainable sensitivity witness, not an optimal attack, a lower bound on necessary changes, or a certificate of robustness.\",\npaper_strategy.json:200:    \"Quantitative plots must be regenerated from recorded arrays by saved scripts and exported as vector PDF plus PNG at 300 DPI; LaTeX must reference the PDF whenever it exists. Use the same four method colors throughout, colorblind-safe markers, abbreviated case labels and shared legends. Derived seed means must be explicitly labeled summaries of the recorded values, never new experimental observations.\",\npaper_strategy.json:202:    \"After references, use clearpage, bibliography, clearpage, appendix, and a substantive Supplementary Information section. Include the separate LLM Usage section there. Keep essential baselines, uncertainty and limitations in the main body. Later authoritative appendix-ranked analyses belong in Supplementary Information without retroactively changing this strategy.\",\npaper_strategy.json:203:    \"Safe related work remains a writing prerequisite: the current bibliography is not a verified reading list. Most retrieval results are irrelevant and metadata records alone do not establish that an original was read. Only use admissible relevant originals actually read and citation keys synchronized with the supplied BibTeX/provenance; if needed, repair bibliography grounding in the appropriate later stage. Do not fill space with unrelated retrieved works or claim comprehensive novelty. Do not infer the prohibited work's identity, use citation expansion, or access uncertain related pages; stop and log any accidental encounter without continuing.\",\nclaims_grounding.json:8:      \"citation_keys\": [],\nclaims_grounding.json:20:      \"citation_keys\": [],\nclaims_grounding.json:31:      \"citation_keys\": [],\nclaims_grounding.json:42:      \"citation_keys\": [],\nclaims_grounding.json:54:      \"citation_keys\": [],\nclaims_grounding.json:65:      \"citation_keys\": [],\nclaims_grounding.json:76:      \"citation_keys\": [],\nclaims_grounding.json:88:      \"citation_keys\": [],\nclaims_grounding.json:99:      \"citation_keys\": [],\nclaims_grounding.json:110:      \"citation_keys\": [],\nclaims_grounding.json:122:      \"citation_keys\": [],\nclaims_grounding.json:133:      \"citation_keys\": [],\nclaims_grounding.json:144:      \"citation_keys\": [],\nclaims_grounding.json:156:      \"citation_keys\": [],\nclaims_grounding.json:167:      \"citation_keys\": [],\nclaims_grounding.json:178:      \"citation_keys\": [],\nclaims_grounding.json:190:      \"citation_keys\": [],\nclaims_grounding.json:201:      \"citation_keys\": [],\nclaims_grounding.json:212:      \"citation_keys\": [],\nclaims_grounding.json:224:      \"citation_keys\": [],\nclaims_grounding.json:235:      \"citation_keys\": [],\nclaims_grounding.json:247:      \"citation_keys\": [],\nclaims_grounding.json:258:      \"citation_keys\": [],\nclaims_grounding.json:270:      \"citation_keys\": [],\nclaims_grounding.json:281:      \"citation_keys\": [],\nclaims_grounding.json:293:      \"citation_keys\": [],\nclaims_grounding.json:304:      \"citation_keys\": [],\nclaims_grounding.json:316:      \"citation_keys\": [],\nclaims_grounding.json:327:      \"citation_keys\": [],\nclaims_grounding.json:339:      \"citation_keys\": [],\nclaims_grounding.json:350:      \"citation_keys\": [],\nclaims_grounding.json:362:      \"citation_keys\": [],\nclaims_grounding.json:373:      \"citation_keys\": [],\nclaims_grounding.json:385:      \"citation_keys\": [],\nclaims_grounding.json:396:      \"citation_keys\": [],\nclaims_grounding.json:408:      \"citation_keys\": [],\nclaims_grounding.json:419:      \"citation_keys\": [],\nclaims_grounding.json:431:      \"citation_keys\": [],\nclaims_grounding.json:442:      \"citation_keys\": [],\nclaims_grounding.json:454:      \"citation_keys\": [],\nclaims_grounding.json:465:      \"citation_keys\": [],\nclaims_grounding.json:477:      \"citation_keys\": [],\nclaims_grounding.json:488:      \"citation_keys\": [],\nclaims_grounding.json:498:      \"citation_keys\": [],\nclaims_grounding.json:508:      \"citation_keys\": [],\nclaims_grounding.json:518:      \"citation_keys\": [],\nclaims_grounding.json:528:      \"citation_keys\": [],\nclaims_grounding.json:538:      \"citation_keys\": [],\nclaims_grounding.json:550:      \"citation_keys\": [],\nclaims_grounding.json:566:      \"citation_keys\": [],\nclaims_grounding.json:576:      \"citation_keys\": [],\nclaims_grounding.json:586:      \"citation_keys\": [],\nclaims_grounding.json:597:      \"citation_keys\": [],\nclaims_grounding.json:611:      \"citation_keys\": [],\nclaims_grounding.json:623:      \"citation_keys\": [],\nclaims_grounding.json:636:      \"citation_keys\": [],\nclaims_grounding.json:648:      \"citation_keys\": [],\nclaims_grounding.json:659:      \"citation_keys\": [],\nclaims_grounding.json:670:      \"citation_keys\": [],\nclaims_grounding.json:683:      \"citation_keys\": [],\nclaims_grounding.json:696:      \"citation_keys\": [\nclaims_grounding.json:705:      \"citation_keys\": [\nclaims_grounding.json:714:      \"citation_keys\": [\nclaims_grounding.json:723:      \"citation_keys\": [\nclaims_grounding.json:728:      \"claim\": \"Related-work attribution: On Extending the Bradley-Terry Model to Accommodate Ties in Paired Comparison Experiments\",\nclaims_grounding.json:732:      \"citation_keys\": [\nclaims_grounding.json:737:      \"claim\": \"Related-work attribution: Ties in Paired-Comparison Experiments: A Generalization of the Bradley-Terry Model\",\nclaims_grounding.json:741:      \"citation_keys\": [\nclaims_grounding.json:746:      \"claim\": \"Related-work attribution: MM algorithms for generalized Bradley-Terry models\",\nclaims_grounding.json:750:      \"citation_keys\": [\nclaims_grounding.json:755:      \"claim\": \"Related-work attribution: Bradley-Terry Models in\\n                    <i>R</i>\\n                    : The\\n                    <b>BradleyTerry2</b>\\n                    Package\",\nclaims_grounding.json:759:      \"citation_keys\": [\nclaims_grounding.json:768:      \"citation_keys\": [\nclaims_grounding.json:777:      \"citation_keys\": [\nclaims_grounding.json:786:      \"citation_keys\": [\nclaims_grounding.json:795:      \"citation_keys\": [\nclaims_grounding.json:804:      \"citation_keys\": [\nclaims_grounding.json:813:      \"citation_keys\": [\nclaims_grounding.json:822:      \"citation_keys\": [\nclaims_grounding.json:831:      \"citation_keys\": [\nclaims_grounding.json:840:      \"citation_keys\": [\nclaims_grounding.json:849:      \"citation_keys\": [\nclaims_grounding.json:858:      \"citation_keys\": [\nclaims_grounding.json:867:      \"citation_keys\": [\nclaims_grounding.json:876:      \"citation_keys\": [\nclaims_grounding.json:885:      \"citation_keys\": [\nclaims_grounding.json:894:      \"citation_keys\": [\nclaims_grounding.json:903:      \"citation_keys\": [\nclaims_grounding.json:908:      \"claim\": \"Related-work attribution: Chatbot Arena: An Open Platform for Evaluating LLMs by Human Preference\",\nclaims_grounding.json:912:      \"citation_keys\": [\n19:\\section{Introduction}\n20:Pairwise judgments offer a common interface for comparing systems even when an absolute quality scale is difficult to define. A user can prefer one response to another; a game supplies a winner; a statistical model turns the resulting comparison graph into scores and ranks. The Bradley--Terry construction is a familiar example \\citep{Hunter2004}. Human-preference platforms such as Chatbot Arena make these rankings particularly consequential for model comparison \\citep{Chiang2024}. Their interpretation nevertheless requires separating several questions: how uncertain is the ordering under resampling, how well does the model describe a population, and how much can the fitted ordering change when selected observations change?\n42:\\section{Related Work}\n43:\\paragraph{Models and ranking targets.}\n44:Statistical comparison models connect qualitative judgments to latent scales \\citep{Thurstone1927}; tournament-strength estimation also has a long likelihood-based history \\citep{Zermelo1929}. Bradley--Terry fitting and its generalizations have established computational treatments \\citep{Hunter2004,Turner2012}; spectral inference offers another computational perspective on choice models \\citep{Maystre2015}. Explicit tie models introduce additional structure \\citep{Rao1967,Davidson1970}; our fractional-outcome convention instead preserves the fixed estimator under study. Separation can complicate unpenalized logistic estimation \\citep{Albert1984}; our fixed quadratic penalty is distinct from bias-reduced likelihood estimation \\citep{Firth1993}.\n46:Alternative ranking formulations emphasize graph structure and different estimands. Rank Centrality uses a random-walk interpretation \\citep{Negahban2017}, while combinatorial Hodge theory distinguishes globally consistent rankings from cyclic components \\citep{Jiang2011}. We do not compare estimators here. We measure changed full-order relations and report normalized rank displacement as a secondary quantity, related to permutation disarray \\citep{Diaconis1977}. Full-order disruption should not be confused with a top-list comparison \\citep{Fagin2003} or with a change in the identity of the best participant.\n48:\\paragraph{Influence and uncertainty.}\n49:Influence-function analysis studies local sensitivity \\citep{Hampel1974}; influential-observation diagnostics and model-level explanations develop related perspectives \\citep{Cook1977,Koh2017}. Finite-sample robustness analysis asks whether removing a small subset changes a conclusion \\citep{Broderick2020}. Here the allowed intervention is an outcome flip rather than removal, and finite edits are assessed through refitting. We make no comprehensive novelty claim about ranking sensitivity.\n51:Bootstrap methodology provides a separate route to assessing variability \\citep{Efron1979,Efron1986,Davison1997}. Dependence within observations motivates grouped resampling rather than treating every record as independent \\citep{Cameron2008}. Our percentile intervals are conditional diagnostics, not a claim of the bootstrap refinements studied in that literature. More generally, robust estimation distinguishes an ideal model from departures from it \\citep{Huber1964}. The stress tests below concern the observed fitted rankings, without assuming that the model captures all preference heterogeneity or sports dynamics.\n53:\\section{Method}\n54:\\subsection{Problem Setup and Estimand}\n61:Participant scores are centered. The NBA home coefficient, when present, receives the same penalty. This is a sum-loss objective: neither sample-size normalization nor regularization strength changes after an edit. The fixed penalty makes the objective strictly convex; the implementation uses converged numerical solutions rather than an exact-arithmetic claim. Standard convex-optimization distinctions between an objective and its numerical solution remain relevant \\citep{Bubeck2015}.\n79:\\subsection{Design Rationale: Screen Locally, Assess Finite Changes}\n91:\\subsection{Guided Refit and Complete-Path Selection}\n102:\\subsection{Matched-Budget Comparison Families}\n108:\\section{Experimental Protocol}\n109:\\subsection{Data Designs and Frozen Evaluation}\n138:\\subsection{Aggregation, Resampling, and Completeness}\n153:\\section{Results}\n154:\\subsection{Attainable Disruption on the Observed Panels}\n173:\\subsection{Paired Evidence Is Stronger on Arena Than Across NBA Seasons}\n188:\\subsection{Nonlinearity, Repetition, and Missing Ablations}\n200:\\section{Discussion and Limitations}\n201:\\paragraph{What the measurements establish.}\n204:\\paragraph{Why the cross-design contrast remains descriptive.}\n207:The fixed ridge penalty introduces another cross-panel qualification. Because the loss is summed, the same penalty has different relative strength at different sample sizes. Holding it fixed is necessary for this particular comparison to define an unchanged estimator, yet it does not remove regularization as a possible explanation of cross-panel differences. No penalty sweep or alternative tie-model study is available. Results under explicit tie models, a time-varying sports model \\citep{Glickman1999}, different participant filtering, or another ranking estimator could differ.\n209:\\paragraph{Conditional uncertainty is not selection uncertainty.}\n212:\\paragraph{Ranking scope and deployment interpretation.}\n215:\\paragraph{Evidence boundaries.}\n218:\\section{Conclusion}\n229:\\section{Supplementary Information}\n230:\\subsection{Data Provenance and Preparation}\n241:\\subsection{Inspectable Implementation and Numerical Details}\n253:\\subsection{Uncertainty Target and Interval Interpretation}\n271:\\subsection{Individual Seed Summaries}\n305:\\subsection{Audits, Reproduction, and Unfinished Comparisons}\n319:\\subsection{LLM Usage}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_12","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"sed -n '99,108p' paper.tex; sed -n '198,222p' paper.tex; jq '{scope, documentary_support_policy, claim_count:(.claims|length),sample_claim:.claims[0]}' claims_grounding.json; jq '{profile:.paper_profile,presentation:.presentation_contract}' paper_data.json\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_12","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"sed -n '99,108p' paper.tex; sed -n '198,222p' paper.tex; jq '{scope, documentary_support_policy, claim_count:(.claims|length),sample_claim:.claims[0]}' claims_grounding.json; jq '{profile:.paper_profile,presentation:.presentation_contract}' paper_data.json\"","aggregated_output":"Refinement considers swaps of selected positions and replacements by unused binary rows. Only affected prefixes require rescoring: a swap at zero-based positions $u<v$ changes budgets $u<k\\le v$, while replacement at position $u$ changes budgets $k>u$. Cached exact-refit results are keyed by the sorted original-row prefix. Screening uses the current smooth-potential gradient and the original inverse Hessian; a change is accepted only when its complete lexicographic score strictly improves. A bounded search retains the complete incumbent if refinement is exhausted or fails. This is heuristic selection, not exhaustive optimization. The code supports these operations, but no accepted-swap trace or component-isolating final comparison is available; the results cannot identify which operation explains an advantage.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/flip_search.py#L309-L475]]\n\n\\subsection{Matched-Budget Comparison Families}\n\\textbf{Uniform} selects binary rows without directed influence screening. \\textbf{Degree-stratified} matches the guided prefix's counts within quartile bins of the sum of the two participants' comparison degrees. This approximates exposure matching; it does not fix individual endpoint counts, participant identity, or outcome direction. \\textbf{Small margin} selects rows with the smallest absolute fitted matchup margin. A close matchup is not necessarily a close adjacent rank contrast, so this control tests a different local-selection rule.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/task_contract.json#/method]]\n\nAll methods use the same observed input, estimator, legal edit units, and integer budgets. The random controls average their fixed streams before comparison. They are informative matched-budget controls, not a claim to cover every possible ranking intervention. The guided selector's additional search is part of the compared configuration; these comparisons are neither equal-operation ablations nor evidence that a particular extra operation is necessary.\n\n\\section{Experimental Protocol}\nNo final controlled component ablation is available. The data compare the complete guided configuration with the three controls, not exchange enabled versus disabled under otherwise identical conditions. We therefore cannot attribute the observed gap specifically to exchanges, beam width, current-contrast updates, or exact refitting alone. Nor do the results establish that additional search would improve the endpoint: the internal displacement-first criterion and reported inversion fraction are related but different quantities.\n\n\\section{Discussion and Limitations}\n\\paragraph{What the measurements establish.}\nThe stress tests show feasible ways to change observed fitted rankings at explicit costs. The matched controls demonstrate that the measured changes are not adequately summarized by the cost alone: which outcomes change matters. The degree-stratified comparison narrows an exposure-based explanation, but its coarse matching leaves substantial freedom in participant allocation and outcome direction. The small-margin comparison shows that choosing close observed matchups is not the same operation as selecting edits that affect the full fitted ordering. These are configuration comparisons, not isolated causal explanations of algorithmic components.\n\n\\paragraph{Why the cross-design contrast remains descriptive.}\nArena and NBA differ in participant universes, graph density, sampling mechanisms, absolute edit counts, ties, and nuisance modeling. A common row fraction is not a common information budget or manipulation effort: the smallest sports intervention changes a single game. Resampling units also differ. These observations cannot separate evaluation design from data volume, graph geometry, or score gaps.\n\nThe fixed ridge penalty introduces another cross-panel qualification. Because the loss is summed, the same penalty has different relative strength at different sample sizes. Holding it fixed is necessary for this particular comparison to define an unchanged estimator, yet it does not remove regularization as a possible explanation of cross-panel differences. No penalty sweep or alternative tie-model study is available. Results under explicit tie models, a time-varying sports model \\citep{Glickman1999}, different participant filtering, or another ranking estimator could differ.\n\n\\paragraph{Conditional uncertainty is not selection uncertainty.}\nResampling fixed plans assesses their advantage under grouped reweighting, not the uncertainty of selecting new interventions from new data. Repeating selection inside a bootstrap would target a different procedure; it was not performed. Coarse percentile tails further limit inference. Positive Arena intervals remain conditional evidence, while unresolved sports comparisons prevent a universal comparative claim.\n\n\\paragraph{Ranking scope and deployment interpretation.}\nFull-order disruption weights every participant pair equally and need not change a leading position or quantify utility loss. Top-participant thresholds, supported-ordering intervals, block deletions, annotator-level interventions, and matched human/judge comparisons require evidence absent here. Flipping existing outcomes also differs from submitting new platform votes. Feasible counterfactual edits establish neither attacker access nor misconduct.\n\n\\paragraph{Evidence boundaries.}\nThe supplement supplies inspectable sources and individual seed summaries. Numerical checks do not certify every internal search operation, global optimality, or novelty. All plotted measurements are replayed from final evidence; no new evaluation or component ablation is introduced.\n\n\\section{Conclusion}\nFinite outcome changes and sampling uncertainty answer different questions about pairwise-comparison rankings. Under one fixed, regularized Bradley--Terry estimator, influence-guided exact-refit selection exposes attainable ordering disruption on both a crowdsourced preference panel and sports-season panels. Matched-budget comparisons favor the guided configuration in point estimates, while fixed-plan paired uncertainty supports a much clearer comparison on Arena than across NBA seasons. The distinction between these statements is central: sensitivity is observable without establishing a universally superior intervention or a causal effect of evaluation design.\n\nThe practical implication is to accompany a fitted ranking with an explicit perturbation model, integer costs, simple controls, and a precisely stated uncertainty target. The observed paths are sensitivity witnesses, not optimal corruption thresholds, deployment-access models, or population-validity guarantees. Missing component ablations prevent attribution to exchange alone; unequal graph and data designs prevent causal cross-design conclusions. Stronger claims would require new controlled evidence rather than reinterpretation of the retained aggregate metrics.\n\n{\n  \"scope\": \"Final measurements only; no development-score comparison or causal component attribution.\",\n  \"documentary_support_policy\": \"Implementation evidence paths and locations are substantive inspected support. Registered metric IDs alone do not establish implementation details. Source-copy identity is recorded without claiming an independently reconstructed frozen-tree digest.\",\n  \"claim_count\": 85,\n  \"sample_claim\": {\n    \"claim\": \"Seed 401, arena: integer costs [43, 216, 432]; guided disruption [0.02628968253968254, 0.0873015873015873, 0.11359126984126984]; all matched baseline curves and seed-budget means are reported without new measurements.\",\n    \"source_ids\": [\n      \"final.evidence.episodes\"\n    ],\n    \"citation_keys\": [],\n    \"evidence_refs\": [\n      \"paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes/0/solution/cases/0/counts\",\n      \"paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes/0/solution/cases/0/candidate_curves\",\n      \"paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes/0/solution/cases/0/baseline_curves\"\n    ]\n  }\n}\n{\n  \"profile\": \"standalone\",\n  \"presentation\": {\n    \"forbidden_visible_terms\": [\n      \"eligible\",\n      \"correct and eligible\",\n      \"correctness rate\",\n      \"worker_01\",\n      \"worker_02\",\n      \"combined_score\",\n      \"rubric\"\n    ],\n    \"internal_fields\": [\n      \"correct\",\n      \"eligible\"\n    ],\n    \"preferred_terms\": {\n      \"idea\": \"model variant\",\n      \"ideas\": \"model variants\",\n      \"implementation\": \"configuration\",\n      \"implementations\": \"configurations\",\n      \"program\": \"seeded run\",\n      \"programs\": \"seeded runs\"\n    },\n    \"run_unit\": \"seeded_run\",\n    \"threshold_rate_rule\": \"If a thresholded rate appears in the visible paper, keep one reader-facing meaning per figure, such as target attainment, successful-run fraction, or non-collapse rate. Do not reuse a single label for different task-specific thresholds.\",\n    \"variant_unit\": \"model_variant\"\n  }\n}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
